me; pass `--force` to replace 14 — sandbox user provisioning failed 1  — other error (parse, elevation check, etc.)UninstallKEEP_USERKeep the `srt-sandbox` account, its credential file, and the setup marker. Without this flag they are all removed (the credential is useless without the account and vice versa, so they're treated as one unit)keep-userELEVATED_STEPSet by `uninstall` itself on the elevated process it starts: do the machine-wide removal only. The grants kept for the calling user are that user's to forget, in the process that askedelevated-stepRemove the srt-win WFP filters under the sublayer and the `srt-sandbox` account, its credential file, and the setup marker (unless `--keep-user`). Self-elevates via UAC if not already admin. Best-effort also removes the legacy `sandbox-runtime-net` discriminator group if a prior install left it behind. Unless `--keep-user`, the calling user's persistent write grants (`acl grant --persistent`) are removed first, as by `acl forget --all`, and so is their profile read grant, as by `profile forget`StatusPrint `{user: <user-status>, wfp: <wfp-status>}` as one line of JSON — the same objects `user status` and `wfp status` emit (so Secondary Logon readiness is `user.seclogon`), in a single spawn. The host's readiness check calls this instead of two separate subprocesses. Exit 0UserInspect the sandbox user account that `srt-win install` provisions (and that the sandboxed child runs as)WfpInspect/remove the persistent WFP filtersAclAdd/remove explicit ACEs for the sandbox user on file paths so the sandboxed child can (`grant`) or cannot (`stamp`) read/write them. State is persisted in the shared state per-user session DB so concurrent brokers refcount and a crash mid-session is recoverable by the next `acl` opThe profile read grant: make the calling user's profile readable to the sandbox group, except for credential stores. Opt-in; nothing else in `srt-win` writes it. Runs as the user whose profile it is, without elevationThis session expects loopback open. Must be given exactly when `srt-win install --allow-loopback` installed it: on a difference in either direction nothing is launched and the exit code is 19DENY_READPer-exec read-deny: add an additive `(D;OICI;FA;;;<sb>)` ACE for the sandbox user on `<PATH>` (and a parent `FILE_DELETE_CHILD` DENY) for the lifetime of this exec — under THIS process's PID as holder, released after the child exits. Repeatable. Same chokepoint as `acl stamp`; fails the exec if any path cannot be stamped (per-exec is "deny THIS one command", so a missing path is a caller error, not a skip)deny-readDENY_READ_EXCEPTA path the caller's session is granted (read or write) that lies beneath a `--deny-read` path. While a profile read grant is recorded, a read deny is also written on the recorded paths beneath it; one at or beneath a path given here is left out, as the session's own allow there ranks above a deny set further up. Repeatable. A path that cannot be resolved spares nothingdeny-read-exceptHELD_BYThe process whose session this command belongs to and which is recorded as one (`acl grant --holder-pid`, with or without `--persistent`). Checked: if that process is not alive or is not recorded — or without the flag — this process records itself, under either token, as a session and as the holder of each `--write-root`: the profile read grant is not made or removed beside the command, and no root is removed from under itThe process whose session this command belongs to and which is recorded as one (`acl grant --holder-pid`, with or without `--persistent`). Checked: if that process is not alive or is not recorded — or without the flag — this process records itself, under either token, as a session and as the holder of each `--write-root`: the profile read grant is not made or removed beside the command, and no root is removed from under it.

A command started for a recorded session ends with it: if that process exits while the command runs, the command and everything it started are terminated and this exits **26**. A recorded, live session that cannot be watched fails the command before anything is started.held-byPIDDENY_WRITEPer-exec write-deny — see `--deny-read`deny-writeENV`KEY=VALUE` pair overlaid on the sandbox-user runner's profile environment when building the child's env block. Repeatable. The broker forwards exactly these — it does NOT enumerate its own environment for proxy/CA vars; the caller (whose `generateProxyEnvVars` is the single source) passes them here explicitlyKEY=VALUEQUIETSuppress informational stderr (progress lines, per-exec-deny summary, seclogon-job note). Actual errors still print. The host sets this by default so the sandboxed child's stderr is not polluted with broker chatterWRITE_RESTRICTED_TOKENBuild the child token with the `WRITE_RESTRICTED` flag and the restricting SIDs `[<sandbox user>, Everyone, RESTRICTED]`, so write access held by `BUILTIN\Users` / `Authenticated Users` no longer reaches the child. Off unless passed; the library always passes it. Fails the exec if that token cannot be built — there is no fallback to the ordinary token. Schannel-based TLS clients (System32 `curl.exe`, schannel-backend git, .NET / PowerShell web requests) fail under it. Pair with `wfp verify --write-restricted-token`Build the child token with the `WRITE_RESTRICTED` flag and the restricting SIDs `[<sandbox user>, Everyone, RESTRICTED]`, so write access held by `BUILTIN\Users` / `Authenticated Users` no longer reaches the child. Off unless passed; the library always passes it. Fails the exec if that token cannot be built — there is no fallback to the ordinary token. Schannel-based TLS clients (System32 `curl.exe`, schannel-backend git, .NET / PowerShell web requests) fail under it. Pair with `wfp verify --write-restricted-token`.

Without this flag the exec is refused (exit **20**) while any persistent write grant exists (`acl grant --persistent`): such a grant lets the sandbox account write its tree under the ordinary token. It is refused the same way while a profile read grant exists (`profile grant`), or while either record cannot be read: grants that outlive a session are made for the write-restricted token only.write-restricted-tokenWRITE_ROOTA write root of this command's session, granted with `acl grant --persistent`. Repeatable. The root's capability SID is added to the child token's restricting SIDs, which is what lets the child write there — and only there, among the persistent roots. Fails the exec if no capability SID is recorded for the pathwrite-rootTARGETTarget executable followed by its arguments. Use `--` to terminate srt-win's own option parsingSpawn a process inside the sandboxSpawn a process inside the sandbox.

Two-hop launch: this process (the broker, real user) reads the credential then `CreateProcessWithLogonW`s `srt-win runner` under the `srt-sandbox` account. The runner builds a restricted token (LUA, Admins flipped deny-only, Medium IL, all privs stripped except SeChangeNotify), self-protects, assigns the child to a kill-on-close job with full UI lockdown, places it on a non-interactive desktop, applies process-mitigation policies + an explicit handle whitelist, and waits for it to exit. Propagates the child's exit code.

The child inherits the runner's environment (= the sandbox user's `LOGON_WITH_PROFILE` defaults) with the `--env` overlay applied — proxy configuration is single-sourced by the caller (TS `generateProxyEnvVars`) and passed here via `--env`.

Requires `srt-win install` to have provisioned the user, by a build with this one's setup version; otherwise exits **15**. Typed launch failures print one line of JSON `{"code":…,"message":…}` to stderr and exit with a dedicated code: **16** `mapped_drive_cwd`, **18** `seclogon_disabled` (the Secondary Logon service is disabled or absent), **19** `wfp_loopback_mismatch` (the installed loopback mode is not the one `--allow-loopback`, or its absence, names; nothing is launched — re-run `srt-win install` for this mode).Inside-the-logon half of `exec`. Reads a length-prefixed `RunnerCmd` from stdin and dispatches as the current user — whi